<script>
import { url } from '@abide/abide'
import Doc from '#src/lib/Doc.abide'
import Example from '#src/lib/Example.abide'
import Section from '#src/lib/Section.abide'
import Transcript from './lib/Transcript.abide'
import transcriptSource from './lib/Transcript.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import messagesSource from './lib/messages.ts' with { type: 'text' }
import transcriptTestSource from './tests/Transcript.test.ts' with { type: 'text' }
</script>

<Doc>
    <Section title="A page's bytes grow by the same amount per message">
        <Example
            id="bytes-per-message"
            title="`bytes(of)` — what a render sends, compared across two sizes"
            summary="The transcript is rendered on the server. Its test renders it at 20, 40 and 80 messages and asserts every message adds the same bytes."
            files={{
                'lib/Transcript.abide': transcriptSource,
                'lib/messages.ts': messagesSource,
                'tests/Transcript.test.ts': transcriptTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <Transcript />
        </Example>

        <p><code>bytes</code> measures a payload by its length — a <code>Response</code>, a
        <code>Blob</code>, a string or a buffer — and anything else by the engine's estimate of
        what it holds. One size alone says little, because the document around the messages is in
        it too. Two sizes say how the cost grows, and the growth holds on any machine.</p>

        <p>A transcript that repeated earlier messages in every new one would pass at 20 messages
        and fail here, because doubling the messages would more than double the bytes.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Microtask turns, and objects left behind">
        <p><code>ticks</code> counts the microtask turns a call's result took to settle. A value
        that was already there and is awaited anyway costs a turn at every call site, and this is
        the count that shows it: a value already held settles in none.</p>

        <p><code>retained</code> lists the live objects a body left behind, by type, and
        <code>closures</code> narrows that to functions and their scopes. <code>allocated</code>
        reports what a body allocates per run as a mean over a batch, never as an exact count. All
        three read the engine's heap, and they throw where a DOM is preloaded, because the DOM's
        own objects would be counted with yours. Run them in a process of their own.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/measure/prove-a-change-does-less-work')}>Work gates</a></p>
    </Section>
</Doc>
